LESSONS_S3 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Quotation: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers the Quotation in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the Quotation field walk; apply the rate discipline including the deviation ladder; operate validity and the revision procedure; assemble the offer pack with terms templates; run the pipeline reads; and convert won quotations to Sales Orders.</p><p><b>What a Quotation is.</b> The Quotation is the business's written offer: these items, these quantities, these prices, these terms, valid until this date. Everything downstream — the Sales Order, the deliveries, the invoices — inherits what the quotation states, which is why the offer is built from the registers (modules 1-2) rather than composed freehand: the customer from the gate, the items from the register, the rates from the lists and rules, the terms from the templates.</p><p><b>Where quotations sit in the flow.</b></p><p>1. <b>Upstream:</b> the request arrives from an existing customer, or from a prospect. Prospect and opportunity handling belongs to the CRM lane where the site runs one; the boundary rule is that a Quotation to a prospect still runs through the customer gate before or at conversion — no order ships to a party the register has not onboarded.<br>2. <b>Downstream:</b> the accepted quotation converts to the Sales Order (module 4) — the commitment document. The quotation is the offer; the order is the agreement; the distinction carries through every procedure in this module.</p><p><b>When a quotation is required.</b> Per the written policy: counter and routine trade sales transact without quotations (the standing lists price them); quotations are required for enterprise and institutional business, for anything negotiated, for tender responses, and above the value threshold the policy sets. The requirement exists because a negotiated arrangement without a written offer becomes a dispute conducted from memory.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The sales function prepares and issues quotations; rate deviations follow the approval ladder (Chapter 3); revisions follow the versioning procedure (Chapter 4); and the standing rule for the module: <b>an issued offer is a record — it expires by its date, changes by revision, and is never silently edited.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Quotation field walk. Chapter 3: rates and the deviation ladder. Chapter 4: validity and revisions. Chapter 5: terms and the offer pack. Chapter 6: the pipeline reads. Chapter 7: conversion to order. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Quotation doctype", "12", "<p>Open Quotation (awesome bar) &gt; New. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Quotation To and Party.</b> Customer (the register record) or Lead where the site quotes prospects. Selecting the customer pulls module 1's machinery: the price list, the currency, the terms default, the addresses and contact. The pre-fill is the point — verify it rather than retype it.</p><p><b>2. Date and Valid Till.</b> The issue date, and the expiry date of the offer. Valid Till is a commitment boundary, not a formality: Chapter 4 covers its discipline. It is set per the policy's standard window (and shortened for volatile-cost items), never left blank.</p><p><b>3. Order Type.</b> Sales for the standing case; the other types where the site uses them (maintenance, shopping cart) per their flows.</p><p><b>4. Currency and Price List.</b> Pre-filled from the customer's resolution chain (module 2, Chapter 4). A quotation on the wrong list is wrong on every row; the header check takes two seconds and precedes any row work.</p><p><b>5. The items table.</b> One row per offered line:</p><p>1. <b>Item Code</b> — from the register; the description prints as module 2 entered it.<br>2. <b>Qty and UOM</b> — the quantity in the stated unit, per module 2's unit-column discipline.<br>3. <b>Rate</b> — pre-filled from the list and any applying Pricing Rules; deviations only per Chapter 3's ladder.<br>4. <b>Amount</b> — quantity times rate, per row, readable arithmetic.</p><p><b>6. Taxes and Charges.</b> The tax template per the customer and document context; item-level treatments (module 2, Chapter 7) compute per line. The tax total on the offer is the tax total the invoice will show — quoting exclusive and invoicing inclusive is a dispute generator, and the template discipline prevents it.</p><p><b>7. Terms and Conditions.</b> The T&C template per Chapter 5 — selected, not typed.</p><p><b>8. Totals, discounts, and margin.</b> The document-level discount fields exist and are governed by the same ladder as row deviations (Chapter 3). Before submission, the margin view (where the site exposes it per role) is read: an offer is a commercial commitment, and the preparer confirms it clears the floor the policy sets or carries the approval that authorises the exception.</p><p><b>9. Save, review, submit, send.</b> Draft for internal review per the value threshold; submit to issue; send as the offer pack (Chapter 5). Submission fixes the record — from this point the offer changes only by Chapter 4's revision procedure.</p><p><b>Verification per quotation.</b> The party and list correct; validity set; every row's unit and rate read; taxes per template; terms selected; margin cleared or approved; then the print preview read as the customer will read it, because the document the customer receives is the record the business must honour.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Rates and the deviation ladder", "12", "<p>Quotation rates start at the register's prices and deviate only by decision. This chapter is the discipline.</p><p><b>1. The pre-fill is the default.</b> Rates arrive from module 2's machinery: the customer's list, the per-UoM rows, the Pricing Rules whose conditions the rows meet. For most quotations the pre-fill is the offer, and the preparer's job is verification, not composition: the right list resolved, the expected rule fired (the 50-unit row showing its break), the units matching the rates.</p><p><b>2. The deviation ladder.</b> Negotiated business deviates — and deviations are decisions with owners, per the written ladder:</p><p>1. <b>Within the sales function's band</b> — deviations up to the policy's percentage on standard lines: applied by the preparer, reason noted on the document.<br>2. <b>Within the manager's band</b> — deeper deviations or sensitive lines: approved by the sales manager before issue, the approval recorded on the document (the site's approval mechanism or the documented note per the procedure).<br>3. <b>Above the bands</b> — owner approval, documented. Enterprise negotiations routinely live here, and the approval names the specific concession (the line, the rate, the condition), not a blanket permission.</p><p>The ladder mirrors the credit ladder (module 1) deliberately: prices and credit are the two places a sales document spends the business's money, and both move only with named approval.</p><p><b>3. Concessions are specific.</b> A negotiated concession states its scope: which lines, what rate or percentage, tied to what (the volume, the bundle, the term). Scope matters at revision time (Chapter 4) and at order time (module 4): the concession granted on terminals does not silently spread to scanners in round two, because the approval named terminals. The document's rows and notes carry the scope; the approval file carries the decision.</p><p><b>4. Margin visibility.</b> Where the role's view exposes valuation-based margin, the pre-issue read is standing procedure: the offer's gross margin per line and total against the policy floor. Deviations that breach the floor are exactly the cases the upper ladder bands exist for — the approval is the business deciding, with the arithmetic visible, that this offer is worth winning at this price. An offer issued below floor without that decision is Chapter 9's first error.</p><p><b>5. The deviations read — monthly.</b> Quotations issued in the month with rates below list, listed with line, list rate, offered rate, band, and approver — reconciled against the ladder. The read is the pricing control's offer-side audit, the sibling of module 2's price changes read: list prices move by governed update; document prices deviate by laddered approval; and both leave monthly evidence.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Validity and revisions", "12", "<p>An issued offer binds the business while valid. This chapter is the validity discipline and the revision procedure.</p><p><b>1. Valid Till discipline.</b></p><p>1. <b>Every quotation carries a validity date</b> — the policy's standard window, shortened for volatile-cost lines (imported goods in a moving exchange environment carry the short window the policy names).<br>2. <b>The offer is honoured within its validity</b> — a customer accepting on the last valid day gets the quoted terms; that is what issuing a dated offer means, and the pricing of the offer accounts for the window's exposure.<br>3. <b>Expired offers are not resurrected by editing</b> — acceptance after expiry triggers a re-quote (a revision at current prices, Chapter 4.3), because the costs behind the offer have had time to move and the old document's arithmetic no longer states the business's position.</p><p><b>2. The no-silent-edit rule.</b> A submitted, sent quotation is a record of what was offered. It is never edited in place to reflect a new round — the customer holds the old version, the business would hold a different one, and the negotiation would proceed from two disagreeing documents. Every change after issue is a revision.</p><p><b>3. The revision procedure.</b> Per negotiation round:</p><p>1. <b>The new version is created from the prior</b> — amend or duplicate per the site's convention, carrying the reference to the version it supersedes, so the thread reads in order.<br>2. <b>The changes are the round's changes only</b> — the conceded lines at their approved rates, the adjusted quantities, the revised terms — each within its ladder approval (Chapter 3).<br>3. <b>The superseded version is closed</b> — marked per the convention so the pipeline reads (Chapter 6) count one live offer per negotiation, not one per round.<br>4. <b>The new version issues with its own validity</b> — the window restarts from the revision date.</p><p><b>4. The negotiation file.</b> Enterprise negotiations produce a thread: versions, approvals, the customer's responses. The file — the versions in sequence, each approval against its round, the correspondence — is the negotiation's record, and it answers the two questions that always arrive later: what did we offer when, and who approved the movement. Module 4 consumes this file at order time: the order is checked against the final version, and the file is where any discrepancy is resolved from.</p><p><b>5. The validity read — weekly.</b> Open quotations by days-to-expiry: offers expiring within the follow-up window get their Chapter 6 follow-up before the date; offers past expiry are closed as expired (or revived by deliberate re-quote), so the pipeline holds only live commitments. An open expired quotation is a misstated pipeline and a latent dispute — the read keeps both at zero.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Terms and the offer pack", "12", "<p>The offer is more than its rows. This chapter is the terms machinery and the pack the customer receives.</p><p><b>1. Terms and Conditions templates.</b> Open Terms and Conditions (awesome bar) &gt; New: named templates carrying the standing text — delivery terms, payment terms restated in words, warranty and support statements, the validity restatement, the legal boilerplate the business's counsel approved. The rules:</p><p>1. <b>Templates are the only source of terms text</b> — selected on the document, not typed per quotation. Typed terms are unreviewed terms, and unreviewed terms are what the business discovers it agreed to during a dispute.<br>2. <b>Templates are owned</b> — the register row names the owner (the owner or counsel for legal text) and the review date; changes are governed with effective dates.<br>3. <b>Template per context</b> — the standard trade template, the enterprise template with its support and training clauses, the tender template per the tender's requirements. The document selects the context's template; deviations for one negotiation are the negotiation file's documented exception, approved like any concession.</p><p><b>2. The offer pack.</b> What the customer receives, assembled per the policy:</p><p>1. <b>The quotation print</b> — the document on the letterhead, per the print format the site standardises. The print is read before sending, as the customer will read it: rows, units, taxes, totals, validity, terms.<br>2. <b>The attachments per context</b> — specification sheets for equipment lines, the company profile for new prospects, the tender's required documents per its checklist.<br>3. <b>The transmission record</b> — the pack sends from the system (the email with the PDF) or is dispatched with its record; either way the file holds what was sent, to whom, when. The sent pack is the offer; the internal draft is not.</p><p><b>3. Print discipline.</b></p><p>1. <b>One standard format per document type</b> — the format the business's documents carry, maintained by the sysadmin lane; per-user format improvisation produces offers that look like different companies.<br>2. <b>What prints is what the record says</b> — no manual PDF editing after generation; a correction is a revision (Chapter 4), regenerated and re-sent.<br>3. <b>The letterhead and registration details</b> — current and correct per the compliance owner, because the offer is a business document carrying the business's legal identity.</p><p><b>4. The terms read — annually.</b> With the pricing review: templates against their owners and review dates; the legal text reconfirmed or updated by counsel; superseded templates disabled; and a sample of the year's enterprise offers checked for typed-terms exceptions that should have been template updates.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The pipeline reads", "12", "<p>Open quotations are the sales pipeline's commitments. This chapter is the reads that keep the pipeline true and worked.</p><p><b>1. The open-quotations read — weekly.</b> All open quotations, by age and days-to-validity:</p><p>1. <b>The follow-up cadence</b> — per the policy: the issue-week contact confirming receipt and questions; the mid-validity follow-up; the pre-expiry contact. Each contact notes on the document or its thread, so the negotiation file (Chapter 4) is complete and any colleague can pick up the thread.<br>2. <b>The expiry hygiene</b> — Chapter 4's read folded in: expiring offers followed up, expired offers closed.</p><p><b>2. Outcome marking.</b> Every quotation ends in a state, marked when known:</p><p>1. <b>Ordered</b> — converted per Chapter 7; the system links the order.<br>2. <b>Lost</b> — marked with the lost reason from the reason code set (price, availability, specification, relationship, no decision), because a pipeline that only records wins cannot answer why it loses. Reason codes are a register set: few, defined, reviewed — free-text reasons aggregate into nothing.<br>3. <b>Expired</b> — the passive loss, closed by the weekly read.</p><p><b>3. The conversion read — monthly.</b> Quotations issued, ordered, lost, and expired across the window; the conversion rate by count and by value; the lost reasons distribution. The read's uses, stated flatly:</p><p>1. <b>The rate trend</b> — a falling conversion rate is a pricing, availability, or follow-up question; the lost-reason distribution says which.<br>2. <b>The value-weighted view</b> — winning many small offers while losing the large ones reads differently from the count alone; both figures are compiled.<br>3. <b>The aging tail</b> — offers open beyond the standard cycle without outcome marks are follow-up failures or marking failures; the read lists them to zero.</p><p><b>4. The deviation-versus-outcome read — quarterly.</b> The monthly deviations read (Chapter 3) joined to outcomes: what the conceded offers won, at what margin, against what the list-price offers won. The read is data for the owner's pricing review — whether the ladder's bands are calibrated, whether concessions are buying volume or merely spending margin — presented as figures with the offers named, per the standing data-not-editorial rule.</p><p><b>5. The pipeline's filing.</b> The reads file with the sales function's records; the pipeline figures feed the sales page the owner reads monthly (the selling track's reporting is consolidated in module 7 alongside receivables); and the quotation file per negotiation remains the one-lookup answer for any offer's history.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Conversion to order", "12", "<p>A won quotation becomes a Sales Order. This chapter is the conversion procedure and its checks.</p><p><b>1. The conversion mechanics.</b> From the accepted quotation: Create &gt; Sales Order. The system carries the party, the rows, the rates, the taxes, and the terms into the order draft — the inheritance is the point: the agreement the customer accepted is what the order states, without re-keying and without re-keying's errors.</p><p><b>2. The conversion checks, in order.</b></p><p>1. <b>The accepted version</b> — the order is created from the final revision, verified against the negotiation file (Chapter 4). Creating from a superseded version ships the wrong round's prices; the file's version thread is the defence.<br>2. <b>Validity standing</b> — acceptance within the validity window (Chapter 4's rule; expired acceptance re-quotes first).<br>3. <b>The customer's standing</b> — for prospects quoted as leads, the customer gate completes now (module 1): the record, the terms and credit per the ladder, before the order submits. For existing customers, the credit check runs at order submission per module 1's machinery — a won offer that exceeds the limit routes to the credit decision, not around it.<br>4. <b>Acceptance evidence</b> — the customer's acceptance (the signed offer, the purchase order document, the written confirmation) attaches to the order. The agreement's evidence lives with the commitment document, because module 4's delivery and invoicing disputes resolve from it.</p><p><b>3. Partial acceptance.</b> The customer accepts part of the offer: the order carries the accepted rows only; the quotation's remaining rows stay open or close per the negotiation's reality (the declined lines marked lost with their reason; the deferred lines carried in a follow-on quotation with current validity). The order never carries rows the customer did not accept, and the quotation's outcome marking reflects what actually happened per line group.</p><p><b>4. Changes at acceptance.</b> The customer accepts with one change — a quantity, a delivery split, a term. The change routes by size: within the ladder's bands, the order draft carries it with the approval noted; beyond them, a final revision issues and is accepted before the order proceeds. The rule is the same as everywhere in the module: the order states what was agreed, and what was agreed has its approval.</p><p><b>5. The handoff.</b> The submitted order opens module 4's territory: the commitment schedule, the reservation of stock, the delivery planning. This module's contribution ends with a clean handoff — the order matching the final offer, the evidence attached, the quotation marked ordered and linked — and the negotiation file closed into the order's record.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: the Montaigne AH offer", "13", "<p>This chapter runs Chapters 2-7 through an enterprise negotiation: Montaigne AH, a chain requesting a POS hardware and setup offer for <b>12 branches — 12 terminals, 12 scanners, 12 printers</b> — plus installation and training. The cycle runs <b>three weeks and two rounds</b>. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the request and the draft (Chapter 2).</b> The request arrives from an onboarded customer (module 1's gate completed at relationship start). The quotation drafts from the registers: the equipment lines from the item register with their units, installation and training as the service lines the register carries, rates pre-filling from the Trade list. Validity set to the policy's enterprise window; the enterprise T&C template selected (support and training clauses standing). The margin view is read; the draft clears review at the value threshold and issues as round one — the offer pack: the print on letterhead, the equipment specification sheets attached, the transmission recorded.</p><p><b>Step 2 — round one's response (Chapters 3-4).</b> Montaigne AH responds inside the validity window: the specification is accepted; the commercial ask is a concession on the terminal lines and a training restructure. The deviation routes per the ladder: the terminal concession sits above the manager's band and goes to the owner with the margin arithmetic — approved as a named, specific concession: <b>the terminal lines at the conceded rate, tied to the 12-branch volume</b>; scanners and printers stay at list, because the approval names terminals only.</p><p><b>Step 3 — round two issues (Chapter 4).</b> The revision creates from round one, carrying the reference: the terminal rows at the approved concession, the training line restructured per the discussion, everything else unchanged. Round one closes as superseded; round two issues with fresh validity. The negotiation file now holds: two versions in sequence, the owner's approval against round two's concession, and the correspondence thread.</p><p><b>Step 4 — acceptance and conversion (Chapter 7).</b> Acceptance arrives in week three, inside round two's validity, as Montaigne AH's purchase order document. The conversion runs its checks: the order creates from round two (the final version, verified against the file); the credit check passes at the customer's limit; the acceptance document attaches to the order. One change at acceptance — a delivery scheduling request — is a module 4 scheduling matter carrying no commercial movement, noted for the order's schedule rather than re-quoted.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the record closes (Chapters 6-7).</b> The quotation marks Ordered with the order linked; the conversion read counts the win at value; the deviations read carries the terminal concession with its owner approval — the quarter's deviation-versus-outcome read will show what the concession bought. The negotiation is answerable from one file: two versions, one approval, the acceptance, the order. Module 4 opens with this order — the commitment, its schedule, and the delivery waves across the 12 branches.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in quotations, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the below-floor offer without a decision.</b> Symptom: offers issued under the margin floor by composition rather than approval; concessions nobody authorised. Correction: the deviations read run, the ladder applied retroactively where defensible, the offers re-quoted where not. Prevention: pre-fill as the default, the ladder for every deviation, the margin read before issue.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the silently edited offer.</b> Symptom: a sent quotation edited in place; the customer and the business holding disagreeing documents; the dispute conducted from two versions. Correction: the record reconstructed from the transmission file; a clean revision issued. Prevention: the no-silent-edit rule — every post-issue change is a referenced revision.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the immortal offer.</b> Symptom: quotations without validity or expired ones honoured months later at stale prices; the pipeline full of dead offers. Correction: the validity read run, expired offers closed, resurrections re-quoted at current prices. Prevention: Valid Till on every offer and the weekly read.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the spreading concession.</b> Symptom: a concession approved for one line appearing on others in the next round; approvals treated as blanket permissions. Correction: the revision corrected against the approval's named scope. Prevention: concessions specific — line, rate, condition — and revisions checked against the approval file.</p><p><b>Error 5 — typed terms.</b> Symptom: per-quotation terms text; commitments counsel never reviewed; support obligations discovered at dispute. Correction: the offers sampled, exceptional terms escalated, templates updated where the exception was right. Prevention: templates as the only terms source, owned and reviewed.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the unmarked pipeline.</b> Symptom: quotations without outcomes; conversion rates uncomputable; lost reasons unknown. Correction: the tail worked to zero with outcomes marked. Prevention: the weekly open read, the reason code set, and the monthly conversion read.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the Quotation walk including the header checks, the row discipline, and the pre-issue verification; apply the pre-fill-first rate discipline with the deviation ladder and specific concessions; operate validity and the revision procedure with the negotiation file; assemble the offer pack from owned templates with print discipline; run the pipeline reads — open, outcomes, conversion, deviation-versus-outcome; and convert with the four checks, partial acceptance, and the clean handoff.</p><p><b>Module rule 3: an issued offer is a record — built from the registers, deviating only by the ladder's named approvals, valid to its date, changed only by referenced revision, sent as the pack the file preserves, and converted to order only from the final accepted version with its evidence attached.</b> Module 4 covers Sales Orders.</p>"),
]

